title: Crate, tin, and cat
story: fernhill
continues: cellar-dark
description: The tool chain — crowbar opens the crate, the crate holds the tin
  opener, the tin opener opens the kipper, the kipper buys the cat. Every gate
  needs its instrument named.

---

> up
[OK: contains "Cellar Stairs"]

> south
[OK: contains "Entrance Hall"]

> south
[OK: contains "Fountain Court"]

> east
[OK: contains "Boiler Shed"]

# Holding the crowbar is not enough — the same explicit-instrument rule that
# fuse-lose.transcript turns into a death.
> open the nailed crate
[OK: contains "You need something to open the nailed crate with"]
[EVENT: false, type="if.event.opened"]

> open the nailed crate with the crowbar
[OK: contains "You open the nailed crate"]
[OK: contains "Inside the nailed crate you see tin opener, brass valve handle"]

> take the tin opener
[OK: contains "You take the tin opener from the nailed crate"]

# The brass valve handle is `sized for the boiler's water valve` and is never
# needed — the stopcock turns bare-handed in machine.transcript. A red herring
# that reads like a required part.
> take the brass valve handle
[OK: contains "You take the brass valve handle from the nailed crate"]

> west
[OK: contains "Fountain Court"]

> north
[OK: contains "Entrance Hall"]

> east
[OK: contains "Kitchen"]

# Smoke is already following, and has been since the first time the player
# entered the Kitchen — see the note on `feed smoke` below.
[OK: contains "The Smoke follows you"]

> open the pantry door
[OK: contains "You open the pantry door"]

> north
[OK: contains "Pantry"]
[OK: contains "With the door shut, no one in the kitchen would know you were here"]

> open the kipper tin with the tin opener
[OK: contains "You open the kipper tin"]
[OK: contains "Inside the kipper tin you see kipper"]

> take the kipper
[OK: contains "You take the kipper from the kipper tin"]

# `the actor must have its food` — the trait's own gate, and the one place in
# the story where holding the item IS sufficient.
#
# STORY-DESIGN FINDING: the reward line promises "from now on, you have a
# shadow", but `a person, follower` has made Smoke follow since the first
# Kitchen visit, unfed. The entire chain — cellar, crowbar, crate, opener, tin
# — buys a sentence and the `smoke-nose` line in the Greenhouse, nothing else.
> feed smoke
[OK: contains "The kipper vanishes with terrifying efficiency"]
[OK: contains "From now on, you have a shadow"]

# `it must be peckish: already-fed-cat`.
> feed smoke
[OK: contains "A slow blink. The account is already settled"]
